WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.068
Input validation is an important supporting control.

2
00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:09.937
It checks type, range, length, format, allowed values, and business rules
before data reaches deeper application logic.

3
00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:17.940
Validation can reject impossible dates, oversized strings, unsupported
choices, invalid identifiers, and inconsistent workflow states.

4
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.408
It improves clarity and reduces ambiguity.

5
00:00:20.408 --> 00:00:26.477
But validation should support safe query construction, not replace
parameterized queries or safe ORM use.

6
00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:30.879
Authorization must happen before database operations expose or change
data.

7
00:00:30.879 --> 00:00:39.882
A query can be perfectly parameterized and still return records the caller
should not see if the application forgets tenant boundaries or object-
level checks.

8
00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:45.684
Secure database access combines safe query construction with resource-
level permission decisions.

9
00:00:45.684 --> 00:00:53.287
The application should know who the caller is, what action is requested,
which resource is affected, and whether the context allows it.

10
00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:56.355
Least privilege reduces damage when something fails.

11
00:00:56.355 --> 00:01:01.824
The database account used by an application should have only the
permissions needed for its role.

12
00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:05.292
A reporting service may need read access but not write access.

13
00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:10.828
A background job may need access to a specific schema but not
administrative database privileges.

14
00:01:10.828 --> 00:01:16.096
Separating accounts by function can limit impact and make unusual behavior
easier to notice.

15
00:01:16.096 --> 00:01:21.032
Validation, authorization, and least privilege are not substitutes for
safe queries.

16
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:24.700
They are layers that reduce risk around safe query construction.

17
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:34.704
If a developer mixes untrusted data into SQL structure, validation may
miss a future context, authorization may occur too late, and a privileged
account may amplify the problem.

18
00:01:34.704 --> 00:01:42.840
A strong design uses all of these controls together: validate inputs,
enforce access, use least privilege, and build queries safely by default.
